  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "If $tan\u2061x=3,$ then $tan\u2061(-x)=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933873/assets/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{\u2218}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan\u2061x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan\u2061(-x)=-tan\u2061x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan\u2061(-x) & =-tan\u2061x \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
